{"name":"Little Theatre","hex":"#73778f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"little","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Small, not large, limited, particularly.","Small, limited in size."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Not much.","Not at all."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/little"},{"term":"theatre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of theater.","A cinema; movie theatre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/theatre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Royal Standard Theatre","blocks":[{"text":"The Royal Standard Theatre, often referred to as the Standard Theatre was a small playhouse in Sydney, Australia, situated at 223 Castlereagh Street, near the Bathurst Street corner between Bathurst and Liverpool streets, and next to the fire station. \nIt was later known as the Little Theatre and The Playhouse, and was demolished in the early 1920s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg/330px-Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Standard_Theatre","wikidata":"Q108670565"},{"title":"Little Theatre (Rochester, New York)","blocks":[{"text":"The Little Theatre, commonly known as The Little, is a movie theatre located on East Avenue in downtown Rochester, New York, and a non-profit multiplex specializing in art film, including independent and foreign productions outside the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/LittleTheatreRochesterNewYork.JPG/330px-LittleTheatreRochesterNewYork.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Theatre_(Rochester%2C_New_York)","wikidata":"Q6652200"},{"title":"Little Theatre Movement","blocks":[{"text":"As the new medium of cinema was beginning to replace theater as a source of large-scale spectacle, the Little Theatre Movement developed in the United States around 1912. The Little Theatre Movement served to provide experimental centers for the dramatic arts, free from the standard production mechanisms used in prominent commercial theaters. In several large cities, beginning with Chicago, Boston, Seattle, and Detroit, companies formed to produce more intimate, non-commercial, non-profit-centered, and reform-minded entertainments."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Theatre_Movement","wikidata":"Q6652208"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Royal_Standard_Theatre.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Royal_Standard_Theatre.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Royal_Standard_Theatre.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Royal_Standard_Theatre.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Royal Standard Theatre, shown in February 1923, shortly before its demolition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoyal_Standard_Theatre.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg/500px-Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg/1280px-Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg/500px-Carved_facade_of_Royal_Standard_Theatre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carved facade – the Order of Royal Foresters 1884 – in April 1923, just prior to demolition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/